# XSH tss_create()
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.
#
# thrd_error from tss_create() cannot be forced and is not tested.

assertion  XSH.tss_create.001
anchor     functions/tss_create.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/tss_create.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      2024
says       tss_create() returns thrd_success, with a destructor and without;
           tss_get() on the new key in the creating thread returns a null
           pointer.
test       t/001-creates.c

assertion  XSH.tss_create.002
anchor     functions/tss_create.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2024
says       After tss_create(), a thread that already existed sees a null
           value for the key, and so does a thread created after the creator
           had set a value for it.
test       t/002-null-everywhere.c

assertion  XSH.tss_create.003
anchor     functions/tss_create.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/thrd_exit.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2024
says       A thread that ends with a non-null value for a key calls the key's
           destructor once with that value; a thread whose value is null, or
           was set back to null, calls none.
test       t/003-destructor-at-exit.c

assertion  XSH.tss_create.004
anchor     functions/tss_create.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2024
says       A process that calls exit(0) from its initial thread with a non-
           null value for a key that has a destructor does not call the
           destructor.
note       The destructor would write a byte to a pipe; the parent expects
           none before end of file (waiting up to 10 s, then killing the
           child: FAIL).
test       t/004-no-dtor-at-termination.c

assertion  XSH.tss_create.005
anchor     functions/tss_create.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/tss_create.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      2024
says       Two keys created by tss_create() keep separate values in one
           thread, and at its exit each key's destructor is called once with
           its own value.
test       t/005-two-keys.c
